+# Styling the Daily Grind
+
+`index.html` loads `styles.css` directly. The stylesheet uses native CSS nesting
+and cascade layers; it needs no preprocessor or build step. It targets modern
+browsers with support for those features.
+
+The layer order is declared once: `tokens`, `reset`, `base`, `layout`,
+`components`. Later layers take precedence over earlier ones. Keep additions in
+the appropriate layer: unlayered rules outrank all these layers. Avoid inline
+styles, IDs for styling, and `!important`.
+
+Define shared values in the top-level `:root`. Palette primitives describe
+literal colors; semantic tokens describe their purpose. Typography, spacing,
+radii, motion, and layout values live alongside them. Change semantic tokens to
+theme the site. Keep values specific to one component, such as `--hero-overlay`,
+on that component instead.
+
+Classes establish component boundaries. Nest related states and direct-child
+selectors under their owning component. `:where()` makes structural filters and
+states contribute no extra specificity, so component selectors remain easy to
+override. Direct-child matching prevents heading and link rules from leaking
+into embedded components. This page does not need proximity-based theme scopes
+or subtree exclusions, so it does not require `@scope`.
+
+Use logical properties for directional spacing and sizing. Keep resets targeted
+to the elements in use rather than applying a universal reset. Preserve visible
+keyboard focus and respect reduced-motion preferences when adding interactions.
